Exactly. Variable speed, not one speed.

Open ground: move fast.
Obstacle: slow enough to read it correctly.
Through the obstacle: smooth execution.
Clear again: accelerate immediately.

That is better than treating “slow is smooth” like a universal brake. It’s really a terrain-dependent control law.

Speed where uncertainty is low. Precision where error cost is high.

That applies to thinking too: don’t spend ten minutes polishing a straight line. Save the deliberate attention for the choke points.